  "query_preview": "..An upper trough continues to deepen of the\nCalifornia coast this evening, with Utah under a southwesterly\nflow aloft. Moisture has increased today, having been drawn up\nfrom what is now post-tropical cyclone Odalys, with PWATs ranging\nfrom around 0.6 inches across northern Utah to close to inch \nacross the southwest. Meanwhile, hurricane Polo continues to \nslowly approach the Baja coast. This hurricane will contribute \nadditional moisture which will continue spread north tonight\nthrough Monday. This moisture will interact with the upstream \ntrough, which will evolve into a closed low across central \nCalifornia Monday before tracking into Arizona Tuesday, to produce\nwidespread and locally heavy precipitation, especially across \nsouthern, central and eastern Utah. An area of diffluent flow will\nbe maximized across southern and eastern Utah on Monday.\n\nModels continue to show substantial precipitation amounts. The\nlatest NBM has a 75-95% chance of 0.5 inches or greater across\nsouthern through east-central Utah for the Monday through Tuesday\ntimeframe, with a 70-80% chance of 1 inch or greater across much \nof the southern quarter of Utah. Amounts decrease farther north, \nbut the Wasatch Front is still likely to see at least 0.1 inches.\nHeavy rain will bring the potential for flooding/flash flooding, \nand a Flood Watch is in effect for the southern and central Utah \nmountains and southern through east-central valleys from 12z \nMonday through 12z Tuesday. There remains a Moderate Risk for \nExcessive Rainfall across southern Utah Monday, with a Slight Risk\nextending through central and eastern Utah. On Tuesday, there is \na Slight Risk across southern Utah. \n\nThere are still some nuances to where the greatest threat for any\nsignificant flash flooding will be. On Monday, while the plume of\ndeeper moisture will extend across southeast Utah, cloud cover \nmay limit instability there resulting in potentially more \nstratiform rain with just some embedded convective elements. \nGreater instability is expected across southwest Utah, where \nstronger convection may result in higher rain rates more conducive\nto flash flooding, but precipitation coverage could be more \nscattered. Lingering moisture on Tuesday will maintain an elevated\nflood threat, but with the track of the low across \ncentral/southern Arizona, there is some question regarding how \nmuch dynamics is available to aid in the development of \nprecipitation. Some models are now a bit less enthusiastic with \nthe coverage of precipitation by Tuesday afternoon. \n\nThe storm system will exit the area on Wednesday, with high\npressure building in behind it. Cooler temperature on Monday and\nTuesday will start to gradually rebound mid to late week. Drier \nand more stable conditions are expected through the end of the \nweek and potentially through the weekend.",
